## 1. Workflow

![Job Match workflow](docs/workflow.png)

<details>
<summary>Same diagram as Mermaid (source: <code>docs/workflow.mmd</code>)</summary>

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  A["📄 Resume<br/>.pdf / .docx / .txt / .md"] --> B["1 · Parse to text"]
  B --> C["2 · LLM structured extraction<br/>Claude → CandidateProfile<br/>+ search queries 中文/EN"]
  C --> D1["104 collector"]
  C --> D2["Cake collector"]
  C --> D3["LinkedIn collector<br/>(optional)"]
  D1 --> E["3 · Normalize + dedupe"]
  D2 --> E
  D3 --> E
  E --> F["4 · Retrieval<br/>embeddings + BM25 → top 40"]
  F --> G["Fetch full descriptions<br/>(top 40 only)"]
  G --> H["5 · LLM rerank / judge<br/>score 0–100 + reason"]
  H --> I["🎯 Top 10"]
```
</details>

| Stage | What happens | Code |
|---|---|---|
| **1. Parse** | Read the resume file and turn it into clean plain text. | [resume_loader.py](src/jobmatch/resume_loader.py) |
| **2. Understand the resume** | Claude extracts a structured profile (skills, titles, seniority, years, industries, languages, location) and writes search keywords in **Traditional Chinese** (for 104 / Cake) and **English** (for LinkedIn). | [profiler.py](src/jobmatch/profiler.py) |
| **3. Collect jobs** | Each platform collector runs the keywords (in parallel, with polite rate limits and a 24 h cache). Results are normalized into one `Job` format and duplicates removed. If one platform fails, the others continue. | [collectors/](src/jobmatch/collectors/) |
| **4. Retrieve** | Every job is compared with your profile using embeddings (meaning) + BM25 (exact keywords). The best 40 are kept. Their full job descriptions are then downloaded. | [retrieval.py](src/jobmatch/retrieval.py) |
| **5. Rerank** | Claude reads your resume and each of the 40 job descriptions, scores each one 0–100 and gives a one-sentence reason. The top 10 are returned. | [reranker.py](src/jobmatch/reranker.py) |
| **6. Output** | Print the list and save `results.md` / `results.json`. | [output.py](src/jobmatch/output.py) |

---

## 2. AI Techniques

### Used now (Phase 1)

| # | Technique | Where | Model / library | Why |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | **LLM structured extraction** | Stage 2 | Claude (`claude-opus-5-5`), JSON-schema structured output | Resumes have no fixed format. The LLM turns free text into reliable fields, and writes search keywords in the language each platform uses. |
| 2 | **Retrieval with embeddings** (the "R" of RAG) + BM25 | Stage 4 | `BAAI/bge-m3` (local, multilingual) via sentence-transformers; `rank-bm25` | Cheap and fast way to cut hundreds of jobs to 40. Embeddings understand that "後端工程師" ≈ "Backend Engineer"; BM25 rewards exact skills like "Kubernetes". |
| 3 | **LLM-as-reranker / judge** | Stage 5 | Claude, structured output, prompt caching | Reads the whole job description like a recruiter would, catching seniority, domain and must-have gaps that similarity scores miss. |
| 4 | **Retrieve → rerank design** (not a RAG chat) | Whole pipeline | — | We don't build a chatbot or fine-tune a model. A cheap retriever narrows the field, then an expensive but accurate LLM judges the shortlist. |

**More detail on each:**

1. **LLM structured extraction.** The resume is sent to Claude with a JSON schema ([profiler.py](src/jobmatch/profiler.py)). Structured outputs guarantee the reply parses into a `CandidateProfile`, so the rest of the code never has to deal with free-form text.
2. **Hybrid retrieval.** The profile and every job are turned into vectors by `bge-m3`, which runs on your computer at no API cost. The score is `0.75 × cosine similarity + 0.25 × normalized BM25`. Chinese text is split into character bigrams for BM25, so no dictionary is needed. A simple location filter drops jobs clearly in another city.
3. **LLM judge.** Jobs are scored in batches of 10 against a fixed rubric: skills, then seniority, then domain, then location and language. The resume block is identical for every batch, so it is **prompt-cached**, and later batches read it at about 10% of the normal input price. Job text is treated as untrusted data (the model is told never to follow instructions inside a job posting).
4. **Safety net.** If Claude declines a request, the API's server-side fallback retries it on another model automatically.

**Two ways to run Claude** ([llm.py](src/jobmatch/llm.py)):
- **Claude Code login** (no API key needed): job-match runs the Claude Code program in the background (`claude -p --json-schema ...`, with all tools turned off). Usage counts toward your Claude plan.
- **Anthropic API key**: job-match calls the Claude API directly through the official `anthropic` Python SDK.

`JOBMATCH_LLM=auto` (the default) uses the API key when one is set, and otherwise uses the Claude Code login.

**Rough size of one run:** 1 profile call + 4 rerank calls (40 jobs ÷ 10) ≈ 60–90 K input tokens and 5 K output tokens. With an API key on `claude-opus-5-5`, that's roughly **US$0.30–0.45 per run**. Set `JOBMATCH_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5-5` in `.env` to cut that about in half.

### Planned (Phase 2 and Phase 3)

**Data science and analysis**

| # | Technique | Phase |
|---|---|---|
| 5 | Data cleaning: parse salary (月薪/年薪/面議), normalize locations, parse seniority and dates | 2 |
| 6 | Fuzzy deduplication of cross-posted jobs (`rapidfuzz` + embedding similarity) | 2 |
| 7 | Skill taxonomy: synonyms such as k8s → Kubernetes, 前端 → Frontend | 2 |
| 8 | Feature engineering and a weighted hybrid score (skill overlap, seniority gap, salary fit, recency) | 2 |
| 9 | Diversity re-ranking (MMR), so the top 10 aren't all from one company | 2 |
| 10 | Skill-gap analysis: which required skills you have and which you're missing | 2 |
| 11 | Offline evaluation with NDCG@10, Precision@10 and MRR on a hand-labeled set | 3 |
| 12 | Job market report: in-demand skills, salary distribution, top cities and companies (`--report`) | 3 |
| 13 | Job clustering (UMAP + HDBSCAN) with LLM-named clusters | 3 |
| 14 | Feedback loop 👍/👎 → learning-to-rank (LightGBM LambdaMART) | 3 |
| 15 | Score breakdown and explainability | 3 |

**Advanced AI**

| # | Technique | Phase |
|---|---|---|
| 16 | HyDE: embed an LLM-written "ideal job description" instead of the raw resume | 2 |
| 17 | Cascade ranking with a cross-encoder (`bge-reranker-v2-m3`) before the LLM | 2 |
| 18 | LLM job-description structuring (required vs. nice-to-have skills, years, remote) | 2 |
| 19 | Model tiering and cost control (Haiku for bulk work, Batches API, token budget) | 2 |
| 20 | Rubric-based scoring with sub-scores and quoted evidence | 2 |
| 21 | Stronger prompt-injection guardrails for job text | 2 |
| 22 | PII redaction before the resume is sent to the API | 2 |
| 23 | Agentic search loop that rewrites queries when results are weak | 3 |
| 24 | OCR for scanned PDF resumes | 3 |
| 25 | Persistent vector store + "new matching jobs" alerts | 3 |
| 26 | Pairwise (tournament) reranking for the final top 10 | 3 |
| 27 | Application assistant: tailored resume bullets and cover-letter drafts | 3 |
| 28 | LLM observability and prompt regression tests | 3 |

