## 3. Operation Manual (step by step)

> ⏱ First setup takes about **15 minutes**. After that, each run takes about **3–5 minutes**.
>
> Type every command below into the **Terminal**:
> - **macOS:** press `⌘ + Space`, type **Terminal**, press Enter.
> - **Windows:** open **PowerShell**.
>
> Copy a command, paste it into the Terminal, and press **Enter**.

### Step 1: Check that Python is installed

```bash
python3 --version
```

✅ You should see `Python 3.9` or higher (for example `Python 3.9.6` or `Python 3.12.7`).
❌ If you see `command not found`, or a version below 3.9, download Python from https://www.python.org/downloads/ and install it. Then close the Terminal, open it again, and repeat this step.

> 💡 On Windows, type `python` instead of `python3` in every command.

### Step 2: Go to the project folder

```bash
cd ~/Projects/job-match
```

✅ Type `ls` and press Enter. You should see `README.md` and `pyproject.toml` in the list.

### Step 3: Create a virtual environment (only once)

A virtual environment is a private box for this project's packages, so they don't affect anything else on your computer.

```bash
python3 -m venv .venv
```

✅ Nothing is printed. A hidden folder named `.venv` is created.

### Step 4: Turn on the virtual environment (every time you open a new Terminal)

```bash
source .venv/bin/activate
```

On Windows, run this instead:

```powershell
.venv\Scripts\activate
```

✅ Your prompt now starts with `(.venv)`.

### Step 5: Install the tool (only once)

First update pip, the Python package installer. Older versions (such as the 21.x that comes with macOS) can't install this project:

```bash
pip install --upgrade pip
```

Then install the tool:

```bash
pip install -e .
```

✅ The last line says `Successfully installed ... jobmatch-0.1.0 ...`. This can take several minutes, because it downloads the AI libraries.

### Step 6: Choose your AI engine (only once)

job-match needs Claude for two steps: reading your resume, and scoring jobs. There are two ways to give it access. Pick **one**:

| | **Option A: Claude Code login** (no key) | **Option B: Anthropic API key** |
|---|---|---|
| Who it's for | You already use Claude Code (VS Code extension, desktop app or CLI) | You have, or want, a Console account |
| What you do | Nothing. Just be logged in to Claude Code | Create a key at https://console.anthropic.com → **Settings → API Keys** |
| Cost | Counts toward your Claude plan's usage limits | About US$0.30–0.45 per run, billed to the Console |

With **Option A**, job-match finds the Claude Code program on your computer automatically (in `PATH`, `~/.claude/local/`, or the VS Code or Cursor extension) and runs it in the background. Skip to Step 7 and leave the key line as it is.

⚠️ With **Option B**, keep the key secret. Never share it or upload it anywhere.

### Step 7: Create the `.env` settings file (only once)

```bash
cp .env.example .env
open -e .env
```

On Windows, use `copy .env.example .env` and then `notepad .env`.

A text editor opens.

- **Option A:** change nothing. Just close the file.
- **Option B:** replace `sk-ant-put-your-key-here` with your real key, then **save** the file.

The other lines are optional:

```ini
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-put-your-key-here  # keep for Option A; your key for Option B
JOBMATCH_LLM=auto                           # auto | claude-code | api
JOBMATCH_LOCATION=Taipei                    # default city to search
LINKEDIN_ENABLED=true                       # change to false if LinkedIn keeps blocking
JOBMATCH_MODEL=                             # empty = default model (opus / sonnet also work)
JOBMATCH_EMBED_MODEL=BAAI/bge-m3            # local embedding model
```

When you run the tool, its first line tells you which engine it is using: `AI engine: Claude Code login` or `AI engine: Anthropic API key`.

### Step 8: Put your resume in the `resumes/` folder

Copy your resume file into the `resumes` folder inside the project, for example `resumes/my_resume.pdf`.

Supported formats are `.pdf`, `.docx`, `.txt` and `.md`.

💡 No resume handy? Try the fictional sample first: `jobmatch samples/resume.md`.

⚠️ A **scanned** PDF (a photo of paper) has no text the tool can read. Use a `.docx` or a PDF exported from Word or Google Docs instead. For an old `.doc` file, open it in Word and use **Save As → .docx**.

### Step 9: Run the job match 🚀

```bash
jobmatch resumes/my_resume.pdf
```

✅ You will see progress like this:

```
[1/5] Reading resume ...
[2/5] Analyzing resume with AI ...
      skills: Python, Django, AWS, PostgreSQL, Docker
      queries: 後端工程師 Python, 軟體工程師, Backend Engineer
[3/5] Searching 104 / cake / linkedin ...
      243 unique jobs found (104: 60, cake: 60, linkedin: 60)
[4/5] Matching (embeddings + BM25) ...
[5/5] AI reranking top 40 ...

🎯 Top 10 jobs:

1. 範例科技股份有限公司 - [Python 後端工程師](https://www.104.com.tw/job/8abcd)  ⭐ 92  (104)
     Strong Python/Django and AWS match at the right seniority; no Kubernetes experience.
2. ...
```

⏳ The **first run** also downloads the local AI matching model (about 2 GB). This only happens once.

### Step 10: Open your results

Your results are saved in the `output/` folder:

- **`output/results.md`**: the top-10 list with clickable links, plus a table of scores and reasons.
- **`output/results.json`**: the same data in machine-readable form (for spreadsheets or scripts).

On macOS you can open the list with `open output/results.md`.

### Step 11 (optional): Customize your search

| What you want | Command |
|---|---|
| Search a different city | `jobmatch resumes/my_resume.pdf --location Taichung` |
| Show the top 20 instead of 10 | `jobmatch resumes/my_resume.pdf --top 20` |
| Search only 104 and Cake | `jobmatch resumes/my_resume.pdf --platforms 104,cake` |
| Collect more jobs per platform | `jobmatch resumes/my_resume.pdf --max-jobs-per-platform 100` |
| Ignore saved results and search fresh | `jobmatch resumes/my_resume.pdf --no-cache` |
| Save results somewhere else | `jobmatch resumes/my_resume.pdf --output-dir ~/Desktop/jobs` |
| Show detailed logs (for debugging) | `jobmatch resumes/my_resume.pdf --verbose` |
| See every option | `jobmatch --help` |

You can combine options: `jobmatch resumes/my_resume.pdf --location Remote --top 15 --platforms 104,linkedin`.

### Next time you use it

Only three steps are needed:

```bash
cd ~/Projects/job-match          # Step 2
source .venv/bin/activate        # Step 4
jobmatch resumes/my_resume.pdf   # Step 9
```

### 🛠 Troubleshooting

| Problem | Why it happens | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `jobmatch: command not found` | The virtual environment is off | Do Step 4 again, then Step 9 |
| `No ANTHROPIC_API_KEY and no Claude Code program found` | Neither engine is available | Install Claude Code and log in once (Option A), or add an API key (Option B) |
| `Claude Code is not logged in` | Option A, but you're logged out | Open Claude Code, log in, then run again |
| `Claude plan usage limit reached` | Option A, and your plan's limit for now is used up | Wait for the limit to reset, or use an API key (Option B) |
| `Your ANTHROPIC_API_KEY was rejected` | Option B, and the key is wrong or was deleted | Create a new key and paste it into `.env`. Check there are no spaces or quotes around it |
| `⚠️ linkedin: blocked (HTTP 999)` | LinkedIn limits automated access | Wait about an hour, or set `LINKEDIN_ENABLED=false` in `.env`. The other platforms still work |
| `⚠️ cake: HTTPStatusError ... 403` | Cake's bot protection refused even the public job listing | Try again later. Results from 104 and LinkedIn are still used |
| Cake results look unrelated to my resume | Cake keyword search is behind a bot check, so the tool reads Cake's newest-jobs listing instead | Normal. The matching step ranks them, and unrelated Cake jobs won't reach your top 10 |
| `0 jobs found` | The search was too narrow or every platform failed | Try another `--location`, or add `--no-cache` |
| `Cannot read resume` | The file is a scanned PDF, or nearly empty | Save your resume as `.docx` or `.txt` (Step 8) |
| `Old Word format (.doc) is not supported` | `.doc` is the pre-2007 Word format | In Word: **File → Save As → .docx** |
| The first run is slow | The 2 GB embedding model is downloading | Wait. Later runs start in seconds |

